#~
Lantern -- find four relics in the dark and carry them to the exit.

  compile: obc -src gl_lantern.obs -lib sdl2,sdl_gl
  run:     obr gl_lantern

  mouse        look around
  W A S D      walk
  shift        run
  click / E    take a relic you are standing next to
  escape       quit

## Why this program exists

Every other example here demonstrates a feature. This one tries to be a small
game, which is a different test: it uses the newest parts of the framework
together, continuously, under a camera the player controls -- rather than one at
a time in a fixture that was written to make them pass.

The lantern is the point. It is a POINT LIGHT that moves with you, and it casts
shadows in every direction through a cube map. A fixture can check a cube shadow
by putting a caster on each axis and reading six pixels; it cannot check that the
six faces stay consistent with each other while the light moves, because a still
image has no way to be wrong about that. Walking a lantern down a corridor does:
if any face is rotated, mirrored, or fed the wrong view matrix, the shadows tear
or swim as you turn, and you see it immediately.

So the parts under test are: PointShadow with a moving light, Scene collision in
corridors rather than an open room, frustum culling with enough boxes to matter,
and an Overlay HUD that has to be legible over a dark scene.

## What it could not use, and why that is the useful part

Four things this program wants and cannot have. They are marked GAP below, and
they are the argument for the next round of work rather than complaints:

  GAP 1  CLOSED. Writing this program is what established that PollEvents
         handled four event types and discarded the rest, so no mouse button
         existed anywhere in the framework and pickup had to be bound to E.
         GLWindow now reports key and button edges, the wheel and the pointer,
         and pickup is a left click -- with E kept, because a one-button game
         that also works from the keyboard costs one line.
  GAP 2  CLOSED. Rect took no colour at all, so the fuel bar was white and
         "low fuel" had to be spelled out in words. Overlay now has a tint that
         multiplies in the shader, so the bar changes colour per frame without
         re-rasterising anything -- which SetColor could not do, because it bakes
         the colour into the glyph textures and throws the cache away.
  GAP 3  CLOSED. Game.OpenGL still has no audio of its own and does not need
         any -- Game.SDL2's mixer needs no renderer. What was missing was the
         initialisation: nothing opened the audio device, so a working mixer
         looked like an absent feature. GLWindow now takes an audio flag, and
         the pickup chime below is synthesised rather than shipped, so the
         example stays asset-free.
  GAP 4  Translucency, for the relic glow. Every built-in lit shader writes
         opaque alpha and Scene draws in insertion order with no depth sort, so
         a glowing shell around each relic would be wrong from half the angles.

## The one bit of arithmetic worth reading

  fuel_light := @fuel / @fuel_max;
  @lamp->SetIntensity(0.35 + fuel_light * 0.9);

The lantern dims as it burns down, which is the whole difficulty curve: the
shadow map does not change, the light does, and the room closes in around you.
It costs two lines because Light owns intensity separately from colour.
~#

use Game.SDL2;
use Game.OpenGL;
use System.IO.Filesystem;

#~
A relic: somewhere to stand, and whether it has been taken.

This is a class rather than three parallel arrays because the alternative was
@relics[i * 4 + 2], which is how the walkthrough's room started out and is worth
not repeating.

It is NOT a Scene member. Scene holds Box, and a Box is drawn with the scene's
one shared mesh -- so anything that is a different mesh plus a transform, which
is what a relic is, has no home in the framework and has to be drawn by hand
below. That absence is the strongest argument in this program for a Prop type.
~#
class Relic {
	@at : Vector3;
	@taken : Bool;
	@spin : Float;

	New(x : Float, y : Float, z : Float) {
		@at := Vector3->New(x, y, z);
		@taken := false;
		@spin := 0.0;
	}

	method : public : GetAt() ~ Vector3 { return @at; }
	method : public : IsTaken() ~ Bool { return @taken; }
	method : public : Take() ~ Nil { @taken := true; }
	method : public : GetSpin() ~ Float { return @spin; }
	method : public : Spin(d : Float) ~ Nil { @spin += d; }

	#~
	Distance from a point, ignoring height -- the player and the relic are both
	somewhere on the floor, and comparing their eye heights would mean the pickup
	radius changed when you looked up.
	~#
	method : public : GroundDistance(eye : Vector3) ~ Float {
		dx := @at->GetX() - eye->GetX();
		dz := @at->GetZ() - eye->GetZ();
		return (dx * dx + dz * dz)->Sqrt();
	}
}

class Lantern {
	@window : GLWindow;
	@shader : Shader;
	@cube : Mesh;
	@orb : Mesh;

	@wall_texture : Texture2D;
	@floor_texture : Texture2D;
	@relic_texture : Texture2D;

	@camera : Camera;
	@scene : Scene;
	@rig : LightRig;
	@lamp : Light;
	@shadows : PointShadow;

	@relics : Relic[];
	@relic_at : Transform;
	# Reused every frame rather than allocated: the lamp moves with the player.
	@lamp_at : Vector3;
	@carried : Int;
	@exit_at : Vector3;
	@won : Bool;

	@overlay : Overlay;
	@font : Font;
	# Synthesised at startup rather than shipped, so this example needs no asset
	# files. Nil when the machine has no working audio output, which is normal on
	# a CI runner and is not a reason to refuse to run.
	@chime : MixChunk;

	@fuel : Float;
	@fuel_max : Float;
	@radius : Float;
	@sensitivity : Float;

	# Per-frame scratch. Every one of these exists so the draw loop allocates
	# nothing: the framework's *Into forms write into a caller's array, and a
	# Float->New[16] per object per frame is a few hundred arrays a second.
	@view_projection : Float[];
	@mvp : Float[];

	# FPS is turned into a string four times a second rather than sixty. Building
	# it every frame both allocates and defeats Overlay's glyph cache, since the
	# text changes every time it is asked to draw it.
	@hud_timer : Float;
	@hud_fps : String;

	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		Lantern->New()->Run();
	}

	New() {
		@camera := Camera->New(Vector3->New(0.0, 1.6, 9.0));
		@mvp := Float->New[16];
		@relic_at := Transform->New();
		@lamp_at := Vector3->New(0.0, 0.0, 0.0);

		@carried := 0;
		@won := false;
		@fuel_max := 95.0;
		@fuel := @fuel_max;
		@radius := 0.35;
		@sensitivity := 0.0022;
		@hud_timer := 0.0;
		@hud_fps := "";
		@exit_at := Vector3->New(0.0, 0.0, 10.5);
	}

	method : Run() ~ Nil {
		# 4x multisampling, and audio. Both are refused gracefully: the window
		# opens without antialiasing rather than failing, and HasAudio reports
		# whether the device actually opened.
		@window := GLWindow->New("Lantern", 1024, 640, 4, true);
		if(<>@window->IsOk()) {
			@window->GetError()->ErrorLine();
			return;
		};

		leaving {
			Close();
		};

		if(<>Build()) {
			return;
		};

		"Lantern -- four relics, one lamp, and it is burning down."->PrintLine();
		"WASD to walk, mouse to look, E to take a relic, escape to quit."->PrintLine();

		# Relative mouse mode locks the cursor and keeps reporting movement past
		# the screen edge, which is what a look control needs.
		Cursor->SetRelativeMouseMode(true);

		while(<>@window->PollEvents()) {
			@window->BeginFrame();
			delta := @window->GetDelta();

			@camera->Turn(@window->GetMouseDeltaX() * @sensitivity * -1.0);
			@camera->Look(@window->GetMouseDeltaY() * @sensitivity * -1.0);

			Update(Keyboard->GetState(), delta);
			Draw();

			@window->EndFrame();
		};

		Cursor->SetRelativeMouseMode(false);
		GL->ReportErrors("the lantern");
	}

	method : IsDown(keys : Byte[], scancode : Int) ~ Bool {
		if(keys = Nil | scancode < 0 | scancode >= keys->Size()) {
			return false;
		};

		return keys[scancode] <> 0;
	}

	method : Update(keys : Byte[], delta : Float) ~ Nil {
		if(@won) {
			return;
		};

		speed := 3.1;
		if(IsDown(keys, Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_LSHIFT) |
				IsDown(keys, Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_RSHIFT)) {
			speed := 5.6;
		};
		step := speed * delta;

		forward := 0.0;
		if(IsDown(keys, Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_W)) { forward += step; };
		if(IsDown(keys, Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_S)) { forward -= step; };

		strafe := 0.0;
		if(IsDown(keys, Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_D)) { strafe += step; };
		if(IsDown(keys, Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_A)) { strafe -= step; };

		if(forward <> 0.0 | strafe <> 0.0) {
			# Each axis applied separately, so a glancing hit on a corridor wall
			# slides along it instead of stopping dead.
			@scene->SlideMove(@camera, forward, strafe, @radius);
		};

		# An EDGE, not a held state: MousePressed and WasPressed are true for one
		# frame per press. Reading the key as held instead -- which is all this
		# could do before -- fired TakeNearbyRelic sixty times a second for as
		# long as the key was down, and only the already-taken check hid it.
		if(@window->MousePressed(1) | @window->WasPressed(Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_E)) {
			TakeNearbyRelic();
		};

		# The lamp burns whether or not you are moving, which is what makes
		# wandering expensive and the shortest route worth finding.
		@fuel -= delta * 1.0;
		if(@fuel < 0.0) {
			@fuel := 0.0;
		};

		each(i : @relics) {
			@relics[i]->Spin(delta * 1.4);
		};

		CheckExit();
	}

	method : TakeNearbyRelic() ~ Nil {
		here := @camera->GetPosition();
		each(i : @relics) {
			relic := @relics[i];
			if(<>relic->IsTaken() & relic->GroundDistance(here) < 1.3) {
				relic->Take();
				@carried += 1;
				if(@chime <> Nil) {
					# -1 picks any free channel, so two quick pickups overlap
					# rather than cutting each other off.
					@chime->PlayChannel(-1, 0);
				};
				left := @relics->Size() - @carried;
				"Relic taken -- {$left} still out there."->PrintLine();
				# A relic is worth a little fuel, so collecting is also survival.
				@fuel += 12.0;
				if(@fuel > @fuel_max) {
					@fuel := @fuel_max;
				};
			};
		};
	}

	method : CheckExit() ~ Nil {
		if(@carried < @relics->Size()) {
			return;
		};

		here := @camera->GetPosition();
		dx := @exit_at->GetX() - here->GetX();
		dz := @exit_at->GetZ() - here->GetZ();
		if((dx * dx + dz * dz)->Sqrt() < 1.6) {
			@won := true;
		};
	}

	method : Draw() ~ Nil {
		# The lantern is HELD OUT, to the right and a little ahead. That is not
		# decoration, it is the whole feature working or not.
		#
		# A light at the eye casts no visible shadow: every shadow falls directly
		# behind the thing casting it, exactly where that thing hides it. Measured
		# on this scene -- a horizontal scan across a wall with a pillar in front
		# of it read a smooth bright hump, 138 up to 255 and back to 161, with no
		# dark band anywhere. Moving the lamp 0.6 to one side put a hard notch in
		# the same scan, 84 down to 3. Same shadow map, same bias; only the offset
		# changed.
		eye := @camera->GetPosition();
		right := @camera->GroundRight();
		ahead := @camera->GroundForward();
		@lamp_at->Set(
			eye->GetX() + right->GetX() * 0.55 + ahead->GetX() * 0.35,
			eye->GetY() - 0.2,
			eye->GetZ() + right->GetZ() * 0.55 + ahead->GetZ() * 0.35);
		@lamp->SetPosition(@lamp_at);

		# Intensity is low and the range is short, because the readings above also
		# showed the first numbers here saturating every surface to 255 -- at which
		# point a shadow cannot darken anything and the dungeon is not dark.
		fuel_light := @fuel / @fuel_max;
		@lamp->SetIntensity(0.12 + fuel_light * 0.20);

		# Pass one: six faces of depth, from the lamp. This is the expensive part
		# -- every caster is submitted six times -- and it is why the room is
		# built from as few boxes as it can be.
		@shadows->SetLight(@lamp);
		@shadows->Capture(@scene, @window);

		# Pass two: the scene from the eye, reading that cube.
		# Cheap when nothing changed, and correct when it did: a window can be
		# resized at any moment, and a projection built once at startup then
		# stretches the scene while the pixel-space HUD stays right.
		@camera->SetAspect(@window->GetAspect());
		@view_projection := @camera->GetViewProjection();

		@shader->Use();
		@rig->SetViewPosition(eye);
		@rig->ApplyTo(@shader);
		@shadows->ApplyTo(@shader);
		@shader->SetInt("tex", 0);

		@scene->Draw(@view_projection);
		DrawRelics();
		DrawHud();
	}

	#~
	Relics are drawn by hand, one at a time.

	Scene cannot hold them: it draws every member with the one mesh it was given,
	and a relic is a sphere. So this is the five-line bind-transform-upload-draw
	sequence that appears in three other examples in this directory, written out a
	fourth time. A Prop type -- mesh, material, transform -- would delete it from
	all four and give relics frustum culling and instancing for free.
	~#
	method : DrawRelics() ~ Nil {
		@relic_texture->Bind(0);

		each(i : @relics) {
			relic := @relics[i];
			if(<>relic->IsTaken()) {
				at := relic->GetAt();
				# Bobbing, so a relic reads as an object in the room rather than a
				# decal on the floor. The lamp's falloff does the rest.
				bob := 0.12 * relic->GetSpin()->Sin();
				@relic_at->SetPosition(at->GetX(), at->GetY() + bob, at->GetZ());
				@relic_at->SetRotation(relic->GetSpin(), 0.0, 0.0);
				@relic_at->SetScale(0.28);

				@relic_at->MvpInto(@mvp, @view_projection);
				@shader->SetMatrix4("mvp", @mvp);
				@shader->SetMatrix4("model", @relic_at->GetMatrix());
				@orb->Draw();
			};
		};

		# GAP 4: a translucent glow shell around each relic belongs here. Every
		# built-in lit shader writes opaque alpha, and Scene draws in insertion
		# order with no depth sort, so it would be wrong from half the angles.
	}

	method : DrawHud() ~ Nil {
		if(@overlay = Nil) {
			return;
		};

		@hud_timer += @window->GetDelta();
		if(@hud_timer > 0.25) {
			@hud_timer := 0.0;
			fps := @window->GetFps()->As(Int);
			drawn := @scene->GetDrawnCount();
			culled := @scene->GetCulledCount();
			@hud_fps := "{$fps} fps   {$drawn} drawn / {$culled} culled";
		};

		total := @relics->Size();
		# Interpolation reads locals, not instance variables.
		carried := @carried;

		@overlay->Begin();

		# The bar carries the reading twice over: its width, and now its colour.
		# Green through amber to red as the lamp burns down, so the state is
		# legible without reading anything.
		fraction := @fuel / @fuel_max;
		bar := (200.0 * fraction)->As(Int);

		# The trough, so an empty bar is still visibly a bar rather than nothing.
		@overlay->Rect(16, 44, 200, 10, 38, 34, 30);
		if(bar > 0) {
			red := 220;
			green := 60;
			if(fraction > 0.25) {
				# 0.25..1.0 mapped onto amber..green
				lift := (fraction - 0.25) / 0.75;
				red := 220 - (140.0 * lift)->As(Int);
				green := 60 + (150.0 * lift)->As(Int);
			};
			@overlay->Rect(16, 44, bar, 10, red, green, 70);
		};

		@overlay->Text("relics {$carried} / {$total}", 16, 16);

		if(@won) {
			@overlay->Text("You are out. All four, and the lamp still lit.", 16, 68);
		}
		else if(@fuel <= 0.0) {
			@overlay->Text("The lamp is out. You can still feel your way.", 16, 68);
		}
		else if(fraction < 0.25) {
			@overlay->Text("The lamp is guttering.", 16, 68);
		}
		else if(@carried = total) {
			@overlay->Text("All four. Back to the entrance.", 16, 68);
		};

		# Dimmer than the rest, because a frame counter is not what you are
		# playing. One call, no re-rasterising -- SetColor here would throw away
		# every cached glyph on screen twice a frame.
		@overlay->SetTintRgb(120, 130, 145);
		@overlay->Text(@hud_fps, 16, @window->GetHeight() - 30);

		@overlay->End();
	}

	method : Build() ~ Bool {
		@camera->SetPerspective(Float->Pi() / 3.0, @window->GetAspect(), 0.1, 60.0);
		GL->Enable(Capability->GL_CULL_FACE);
		GL->ClearColor(0.015, 0.017, 0.025, 1.0);

		@shader := Shader->LitTexturedPointShadowed();
		if(<>@shader->IsOk()) {
			@shader->GetLog()->ErrorLine();
			return false;
		};

		@cube := Mesh->Cube();
		@orb := Mesh->Sphere(18, 10);
		if(<>@cube->IsOk() | <>@orb->IsOk()) {
			"Could not upload the geometry"->ErrorLine();
			return false;
		};

		@wall_texture := Texture2D->Checker(64, 4, Texture2D->Rgb(96, 88, 78),
			Texture2D->Rgb(66, 60, 54), TextureWrap->GL_REPEAT);
		@floor_texture := Texture2D->Checker(64, 3, Texture2D->Rgb(58, 54, 50),
			Texture2D->Rgb(44, 41, 38), TextureWrap->GL_REPEAT);
		@relic_texture := Texture2D->Solid(0xFFF2C879);
		if(<>@wall_texture->IsOk() | <>@floor_texture->IsOk() | <>@relic_texture->IsOk()) {
			"Could not build the textures"->ErrorLine();
			return false;
		};

		@shadows := PointShadow->New(512);
		if(<>@shadows->IsOk()) {
			@shadows->GetError()->ErrorLine();
			return false;
		};
		# A lamp inside the room is close to what it lights, so the bias that suits
		# a distant sun is far too large here and detaches every shadow from its
		# caster.
		@shadows->SetBias(0.06);

		BuildAudio();
		BuildLight();
		BuildDungeon();
		BuildRelics();
		BuildOverlay();

		return true;
	}

	#~
	A short bell, written to a temporary WAV and loaded back.

	Two sine partials a fifth apart with a fast attack and a long exponential
	decay -- enough to read as "you picked something up" without shipping an
	asset. The file goes to the system temp directory rather than beside the
	program, because the runners start it from the deploy tree's bin and that
	should not accumulate files.
	~#
	method : BuildAudio() ~ Nil {
		if(<>@window->HasAudio()) {
			return;
		};

		path := System.Runtime->GetTempDir() + "/objeck_lantern_chime.wav";
		if(WriteChime(path)) {
			@chime := MixChunk->New(path);
			if(@chime <> Nil & @chime->IsNull()) {
				@chime := Nil;
			};
		};
	}

	method : WriteChime(file : String) ~ Bool {
		rate := 22050;
		count := 5200;
		data_size := count * 2;

		writer := FileWriter->New(file);
		if(<>writer->IsOpen()) {
			return false;
		};

		# RIFF/WAVE, 16-bit mono. Written by hand because there is no WAV writer
		# in the standard library and the header is twelve fields.
		writer->WriteByte(82); writer->WriteByte(73); writer->WriteByte(70); writer->WriteByte(70);
		WriteU32(writer, 36 + data_size);
		writer->WriteByte(87); writer->WriteByte(65); writer->WriteByte(86); writer->WriteByte(69);
		writer->WriteByte(102); writer->WriteByte(109); writer->WriteByte(116); writer->WriteByte(32);
		WriteU32(writer, 16);
		WriteU16(writer, 1);
		WriteU16(writer, 1);
		WriteU32(writer, rate);
		WriteU32(writer, rate * 2);
		WriteU16(writer, 2);
		WriteU16(writer, 16);
		writer->WriteByte(100); writer->WriteByte(97); writer->WriteByte(116); writer->WriteByte(97);
		WriteU32(writer, data_size);

		two_pi := 2.0 * Float->Pi();
		rate_f := rate->As(Float);
		count_f := count->As(Float);
		low := 0.0;
		high := 0.0;

		for(i := 0; i < count; i += 1;) {
			t := i->As(Float) / count_f;
			low += two_pi * 880.0 / rate_f;
			high += two_pi * 1320.0 / rate_f;

			# Fast attack, long decay. Without the attack ramp the first sample
			# jumps from silence and the speaker clicks.
			envelope := (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t);
			if(t < 0.01) {
				envelope := envelope * (t / 0.01);
			};

			value := envelope * (0.62 * low->Sin() + 0.38 * high->Sin());
			sample := (value * 20000.0)->As(Int);
			if(sample > 32767) { sample := 32767; };
			if(sample < -32768) { sample := -32768; };
			if(sample < 0) { sample := sample + 65536; };

			writer->WriteByte(sample % 256);
			writer->WriteByte((sample / 256) % 256);
		};

		writer->Close();

		return true;
	}

	method : WriteU32(writer : FileWriter, value : Int) ~ Nil {
		writer->WriteByte(value % 256);
		writer->WriteByte((value / 256) % 256);
		writer->WriteByte((value / 65536) % 256);
		writer->WriteByte((value / 16777216) % 256);
	}

	method : WriteU16(writer : FileWriter, value : Int) ~ Nil {
		writer->WriteByte(value % 256);
		writer->WriteByte((value / 256) % 256);
	}

	method : BuildLight() ~ Nil {
		# Range, not just colour: a point light with no falloff lights the whole
		# dungeon evenly and there is nothing to carry a lantern for.
		@lamp := Light->Point(Vector3->New(0.0, 1.4, 9.0), 13.0);
		@lamp->SetColor(1.0, 0.86, 0.62);

		@rig := LightRig->New();
		# Barely above black. This is the number that decides whether the game is
		# about the lantern or merely lit by one.
		@rig->SetAmbient(0.035, 0.035, 0.045);
		# Low and broad: dungeon stone is not polished, and a tight lobe on a
		# carried light turns every surface into a mirror pointed back at you.
		@rig->SetSpecular(0.08, 12.0);
		@rig->Add(@lamp);
	}

	#~
	The dungeon: a floor, a ceiling, an outer wall, and enough interior to make
	corridors and corners.

	Corners are the interesting shape here -- for the shadows, because a corner is
	where a moving light's shadow sweeps fastest and any inconsistency between
	cube faces shows; and for SlideMove, because an inside corner is where naive
	collision traps you.
	~#
	method : BuildDungeon() ~ Nil {
		@scene := Scene->New(@cube, @shader);
		@scene->SetLightRig(@rig);
		@scene->SetCulling(true);

		# Floor and ceiling: drawn, but walked through rather than into.
		Slab(0.0, -0.25, 0.0, 13.0, 0.25, 13.0, @floor_texture, false);
		Slab(0.0, 3.25, 0.0, 13.0, 0.25, 13.0, @wall_texture, false);

		# Outer wall, four slabs, leaving the south side open as the way out.
		Slab(0.0, 1.5, -13.0, 13.0, 1.5, 0.5, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(-13.0, 1.5, 0.0, 0.5, 1.5, 13.0, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(13.0, 1.5, 0.0, 0.5, 1.5, 13.0, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(-7.5, 1.5, 13.0, 5.5, 1.5, 0.5, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(7.5, 1.5, 13.0, 5.5, 1.5, 0.5, @wall_texture, true);

		# Interior: two long spines with gaps, and a knot of pillars in the middle.
		Slab(-5.0, 1.5, -6.0, 0.5, 1.5, 5.0, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(5.0, 1.5, -6.0, 0.5, 1.5, 5.0, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(-2.0, 1.5, 1.0, 4.5, 1.5, 0.5, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(6.5, 1.5, 1.0, 4.0, 1.5, 0.5, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(0.0, 1.5, -11.0, 3.0, 1.5, 0.5, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(-9.0, 1.5, 5.0, 0.5, 1.5, 3.5, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(9.0, 1.5, 5.0, 0.5, 1.5, 3.5, @wall_texture, true);

		# Pillars. Small, plentiful, and the best shadow casters in the room --
		# a lantern carried past one sweeps its shadow right around the walls.
		Pillar(-2.5, -3.0);
		Pillar(2.5, -3.0);
		Pillar(-2.5, 5.5);
		Pillar(2.5, 5.5);
		Pillar(-7.0, -1.0);
		Pillar(7.0, -1.0);
		Pillar(0.0, 8.0);
	}

	method : Slab(cx : Float, cy : Float, cz : Float, hx : Float, hy : Float,
			hz : Float, texture : Texture2D, solid : Bool) ~ Nil {
		box := Box->New(Vector3->New(cx, cy, cz), Vector3->New(hx, hy, hz), texture);
		box->SetSolid(solid);
		@scene->Add(box);
	}

	method : Pillar(x : Float, z : Float) ~ Nil {
		Slab(x, 1.5, z, 0.4, 1.5, 0.4, @wall_texture, true);
	}

	method : BuildRelics() ~ Nil {
		# One per quadrant, each behind something, so none is visible from the
		# entrance and the lantern has to be carried to it.
		@relics := Relic->New[4];
		@relics[0] := Relic->New(-9.5, 1.0, -9.0);
		@relics[1] := Relic->New(9.5, 1.0, -9.5);
		@relics[2] := Relic->New(-10.0, 1.0, 8.5);
		@relics[3] := Relic->New(10.0, 1.0, 9.0);
	}

	method : BuildOverlay() ~ Nil {
		Font->Init();
		# The path is relative to the deploy tree's bin directory, which is where
		# the runners start the program. It is duplicated verbatim in two other
		# examples -- an Overlay->WithDefaultFont would end that.
		@font := Font->New("../lib/sdl/fonts/lazy.ttf", 18);
		@overlay := Overlay->New(@window);
		if(@overlay->IsOk() & @font <> Nil & <>@font->IsNull()) {
			@overlay->SetFont(@font);
			@overlay->SetColor(236, 224, 198);
		}
		else {
			# A missing font is not fatal: the game is playable without the HUD,
			# and DrawHud checks for this.
			@overlay := Nil;
		};
	}

	#~
	GL objects first, while the context is still current -- every Free bottoms out
	in a glDelete*, which needs one. The window takes the context and SDL down
	after them.

	Six objects and a fixed order, written out by hand, in every example in this
	directory. A GLWindow->Own registry would do it once.
	~#
	method : Close() ~ Nil {
		# Before the window: GLWindow->Free calls Core->Quit, which takes the
		# audio device down with it.
		if(@chime <> Nil) { @chime->Free(); };

		if(@relic_texture <> Nil) { @relic_texture->Free(); };
		if(@floor_texture <> Nil) { @floor_texture->Free(); };
		if(@wall_texture <> Nil) { @wall_texture->Free(); };
		if(@orb <> Nil) { @orb->Free(); };
		if(@cube <> Nil) { @cube->Free(); };
		if(@shadows <> Nil) { @shadows->Free(); };
		if(@overlay <> Nil) { @overlay->Free(); };
		# The overlay does not own the font -- SetFont says so -- so it has to be
		# closed here. Until Font->Free existed this simply leaked.
		if(@font <> Nil & <>@font->IsNull()) { @font->Free(); };
		if(@shader <> Nil) { @shader->Free(); };

		if(@window <> Nil) { @window->Free(); };
	}
}
